WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:06.480
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and, uh,
today I want to talk about how to study

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.620
effectively, not just how to learn
languages, but how to study effectively.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.550
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.350
on the bell to get notifications.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.210
Uh, I, I'd like to do actually
a series of four videos.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:26.340
Uh, I'd like to talk about how to study
effectively based on my experience

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.925
in learning languages, what I've read
about learning languages, what I've

00:00:29.925 --> 00:00:36.075
read about how the brain works, what I
remember from when I was at school, uh,

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:42.465
some of the things that I think language
instruction does that is not successful

00:00:42.465 --> 00:00:48.165
in schools and why the same unsuccessful
method is used to teach other subjects,

00:00:48.435 --> 00:00:50.474
causing a lot of frustration for people.

00:00:50.745 --> 00:00:54.525
So, first of all, today, I'm
simply going to talk about how

00:00:54.525 --> 00:00:56.655
to study effectively in general.

00:00:57.705 --> 00:01:00.915
And then in the second in the
series, I hope to do a series of

00:01:00.915 --> 00:01:06.705
four actually, in the second series
I'll talk about why I think language

00:01:06.705 --> 00:01:08.955
instruction in schools is not effective.

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:16.365
Then in the third in the series, I want to
talk about, you know, learning in a month

00:01:16.395 --> 00:01:21.645
or, you know, uh, how to learn quickly
and all of the different hints and tips

00:01:22.185 --> 00:01:26.805
and hacks that are offered on the internet
for how to learn languages quickly.

00:01:27.105 --> 00:01:32.805
My views on these, and again, tying it all
back to studying effectively in general.

00:01:33.645 --> 00:01:36.465
And then in the final one, I'm
going to talk about learning

00:01:36.465 --> 00:01:37.825
a language from scratch.

00:01:37.825 --> 00:01:45.270
So again, applying the sort of principles
of effective study to, uh, you know,

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.400
learning a language from scratch.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.680
So, um, I'm not going to talk
about specifically language

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.310
learning or about LingQ.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.460
People complain that I mention
LingQ, although  I love LingQ and

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:58.560
that's  where I learn languages.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:06.000
And what we do at LingQ in a sense as a
reflection of my view of how we learn.

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:08.430
Uh, but I'm going to talk about learning.

00:02:09.134 --> 00:02:09.914
And study...

00:02:09.914 --> 00:02:11.225
effective study in general.

00:02:11.475 --> 00:02:11.835
All right.

00:02:12.825 --> 00:02:17.475
First of all, the first thing I think
we need to do to study effectively

00:02:17.835 --> 00:02:21.525
is to improve our reading skills.

00:02:21.644 --> 00:02:25.845
If we aren't good readers, we need to
become good readers to read faster,

00:02:26.144 --> 00:02:30.690
to get better at acquiring information
through reading, because reading

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.210
is very effective, very efficient.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:38.490
It enables us to access so much
information, whether in, you know, a paper

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:43.860
type of book, a traditional book or an
ebook where we can, you know, look things

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:47.250
up if we're on a niPad or on a computer.

00:02:47.984 --> 00:02:52.155
Being careful to be, you know, to
avoid the distractions granted.

00:02:52.815 --> 00:02:54.525
So reading is super powerful.

00:02:55.065 --> 00:02:57.405
Uh, also get used to listening.

00:02:58.095 --> 00:03:02.055
Uh, again, listening is something
that we can do wherever we are.

00:03:02.625 --> 00:03:05.084
We can enjoy the voice
of the person explaining.

00:03:05.505 --> 00:03:11.114
I find reading and listening
more effective than video because

00:03:11.295 --> 00:03:13.035
with video, we are captive.

00:03:13.484 --> 00:03:15.584
Video can be part of a study plan.

00:03:16.485 --> 00:03:18.195
It can be fun to watch video.

00:03:18.255 --> 00:03:23.685
It's sometimes possible to
explain things via video in a

00:03:23.685 --> 00:03:25.395
way that we can't in a book.

00:03:26.415 --> 00:03:28.905
So I think you need a
combination of all three.

00:03:29.355 --> 00:03:35.235
So when we talk about combining
reading, listening, and video,

00:03:36.165 --> 00:03:37.274
We're talking about combining.

00:03:37.695 --> 00:03:41.535
And I think combining it's
a big part of learning.

00:03:42.435 --> 00:03:47.054
Um, I found that in learning languages,
but not only in learning languages, in

00:03:47.054 --> 00:03:52.575
learning other subjects, I have to sort
of get at the, whatever I was trying

00:03:52.575 --> 00:03:58.320
to learn using different approaches,
different books, different resources.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:02.330
Don't just try to memorize the textbook.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.440
You, you have to try and force
yourself to go out and find other

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.640
resources that cover the same material.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:13.460
Uh, and that's why we combine video
with listening with audio, with, uh,

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:19.190
with reading, because the brain, the
way the brain works is the brain is not

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:25.290
very good at sort of aceing something
now, learning some specific bits of

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:32.220
knowledge or facts, or even, uh, you
know, some, a mathematical equation.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.040
These are things that we kind
of learn them and there's,

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.660
it's difficult for the brain.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:38.950
You know, again, I'm not
a neuroscientist, but.

00:04:39.299 --> 00:04:42.659
They go into the hippocampus and they
don't stay there very long, but the

00:04:42.659 --> 00:04:47.400
strength of the brain is its ability
to sort of start to identify patterns.

00:04:47.729 --> 00:04:53.150
And that's why we have to be patient
and keep feeding the brain, you know,

00:04:53.179 --> 00:04:58.590
the same kind, you know, I've referred
to it as grazing and interleaving, some

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:02.450
of the same material interspersed with
going off and doing something else and

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:08.310
then coming back and reading about this
or in some other format so that this

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:13.650
combination learning, grazing learning is
according to research, much more effective

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.630
than trying to nail something down.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.930
And, uh, that's why it's, it's so
counterproductive in the school

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.640
system where they teach you something,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.690
and now, because they've taught it to
you, therefore they think you should be

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.320
able to reproduce it or be tested on it.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:32.560
And of course you can't.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.430
So you only get six out of
10, which is frustrating.

00:05:36.255 --> 00:05:40.155
As a learner, we have to remember
that however difficult something

00:05:40.155 --> 00:05:45.675
may seem, uh, whether it be a
language or a concept in mathematics

00:05:45.675 --> 00:05:47.325
or in science or anything else,

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:53.790
in time, the brain will develop a
pattern that enables us to grasp the

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.670
concept better and better slowly.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.870
The emphasis is on slowly it and
the Manfred Spitzer, whom I always

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:04.320
quote, he says, you know, the brain
can't do otherwise than learn.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.970
The brain will gradually learn,
create patterns to deal with.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:11.780
All the stimulus and, and
information that's coming at it.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:15.650
It'll drop some of the details,
but it'll start forming a pattern.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:20.150
And when you have this pattern in your
brain, it becomes easier then to remember

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:24.290
some of the details, because you have
a context, the brain has created this,

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:30.659
this, uh, network of, of, uh, patterns
that makes it then easier to learn.

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:35.450
So it's very important not to be
frustrated if we read this explanation

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:39.270
five times and it still doesn't make
sense, or we keep forgetting things.

00:06:39.719 --> 00:06:42.659
And of course we get six out
of 10 or three out of 10 on

00:06:42.659 --> 00:06:46.739
the test and we shouldn't allow
ourselves to be discouraged.

00:06:46.919 --> 00:06:49.799
Of course the education
system is set up that way.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:54.405
It's set up that way because for
whatever reason, the people in charge

00:06:54.405 --> 00:06:57.284
of education feel that they have to
test you at every step of the way to

00:06:57.284 --> 00:06:58.755
make sure you're learning something.

00:06:59.505 --> 00:07:04.604
In reality, what was taught yesterday
may not click in for another six months.

00:07:04.635 --> 00:07:08.594
So it's kind of meaningless to test
us immediately on, on what's in our

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:14.685
short term memory, for example, rather
we should, you know, as teachers

00:07:14.685 --> 00:07:18.705
encourage learners to cover the same
ground using different materials

00:07:18.735 --> 00:07:20.835
and we as learners should resist

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.260
this sort of sense of frustration that we
can't understand, that we can't remember

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.240
it, we can't do it, it doesn't make sense.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:28.830
We have to continue.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:33.480
And that's again, always, I go
back to this idea of combining

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:37.440
things, watch a video by all means,
but then also if you can find...

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:42.555
and in the case of science and math you
have Khan Academy, which is tremendous,

00:07:42.855 --> 00:07:46.725
but you should have still at the same
time, work on your reading, work on

00:07:46.935 --> 00:07:49.005
reading explanations of the same thing.

00:07:49.095 --> 00:07:53.955
Again, without that sense of, you
know, frustration or fear as you're

00:07:53.955 --> 00:07:58.125
not, still not able to remember
or grasp at all, just keep going.

00:07:58.245 --> 00:08:00.615
It's slowly going to sock into place.

00:08:00.795 --> 00:08:03.645
Just the way languages
slowly sock into place.

00:08:04.095 --> 00:08:08.370
And, you know, work on all the different
aspects of your brain, including

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:10.350
the emotional side of your brain.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:13.200
So if you can listen to something that's
pleasant, where you enjoy the voice,

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:17.730
if you don't like listening to your
professor, but if you can find some other

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:23.130
audio, which is more interesting or where
you enjoy the voice, listen to that, but

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:26.610
you still have to go back and read or
listen to what the professor had to say.

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:30.000
Cause you gotta, you gotta,
you know, write the exam.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.255
And most professors like to have
things come back to them the way

00:08:33.255 --> 00:08:35.775
they, they, uh, you know, taught it.

00:08:36.885 --> 00:08:41.914
But, so, so as, as, as a general
principle in, in learning, I think all

00:08:41.914 --> 00:08:45.425
of the things that I say about language
learning, when I think back, you know,

00:08:45.455 --> 00:08:49.595
when I did well in a subject it's because
I got interested in the subject, I read

00:08:49.595 --> 00:08:51.965
up on it using different resources.

00:08:52.445 --> 00:08:57.315
Um, and, and so, and the other thing too
is as with language learning, of course,

00:08:57.315 --> 00:09:02.145
it's good to talk and exchange views
about a subject that you're learning and

00:09:02.145 --> 00:09:06.195
to talk to the other people in the class
and to talk to the teacher and so forth.

00:09:06.225 --> 00:09:09.645
But you have to have something in
you before you can talk about the

00:09:09.645 --> 00:09:12.765
subject just as with language,
you can't begin by speaking.

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:16.290
You have to begin by allowing
your brain to get used to it.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:20.199
And that's where the reading and the
listening primarily, and to some extent

00:09:20.699 --> 00:09:23.310
video is kind of the prerequisite.

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:28.110
And once you've got some of those patterns
in your brain, then yeah, the more you

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:33.180
can talk about it and do experiments
and do things and activate some of these

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:36.000
things that you have brought into your
brain, the better you're going to do it.

00:09:36.405 --> 00:09:41.324
So that's sort of a general discussion
on how to study effectively.

00:09:41.745 --> 00:09:46.005
Uh, the big message is to my mind
is don't allow yourself to get

00:09:46.005 --> 00:09:51.074
frustrated, get hung up at your
mistakes or perhaps your, your results

00:09:51.074 --> 00:09:53.055
on a test or things that you forget.

00:09:53.745 --> 00:09:57.465
Try to find a way to get
interested in, in the subject.

00:09:57.915 --> 00:10:00.735
Uh, and that may mean that
your textbook is boring.

00:10:00.765 --> 00:10:02.265
Find some other materials.

00:10:02.729 --> 00:10:07.410
And graze around and cover the
same ground in different ways.

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:09.030
And I think that's an
effective way to study.

